WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.680
Follow Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter and Instagram!

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.220
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:18.420
A couple of months back I made a video about whether English is really a Germanic language or not.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.960
And I made the rather controversial statement that

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.940
in a sense, English is a hybrid language because so much of its vocabulary comes from the Romance languages and other languages.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:32.400
That statement was controversial because the core of English is indeed Germanic.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.920
And, it is actually possible to speak English with mostly or even entirely Germanic words.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:43.300
For example, instead of saying "literature" (which is a Romance word) you could say "bookcraft".

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.600
Or, instead of saying "ancestor" you could say "forekind".

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.040
It's not exactly easy to speak that way all the time

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.160
but it is possible.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:57.960
There is actually a movement to create and to promote a new form of English like this that stripped off all of its non-Germanic influences

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:05.100
so that English would basically became the language that it would have been if it had not been influenced by Romance languages and by Greek and others.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.000
Some people refer to this strictly-Germanic form of English as "Anglish"

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.480
because it's supposed to take English back to its Anglo-Saxon roots.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.560
Anglish is a form of English "linguistic purism".

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.100
Anglish seeks to achieve the Germanic "purity" of English in a few different ways:

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.420
First, by choosing existing Germanic words rather than their Romance equivalents.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.320
Two; by reviving obsolete Germanic words.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:31.260
Three; by adopting words from Old English or other Germanic languages.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:35.540
Four; by creating new words based on Old English or other Germanic roots.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.280
So take this simple sentence:

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.500
"The man forgot his rainshade at home."

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.200
What do you think "rainshade" means?

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.420
Something that provides shade from the rain.

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:45.720
If you guessed "umbrella", that's right.

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:50.780
"Rainshade" is a new word created from 'rain' and 'shade' and is based on the word 'sunshade'.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.720
"Umbrella" is a loanword from the Italian "ombrella".

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.580
All of the other words in this sentence are regular English words of Germanic origin.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:02:04.100
And you probably noticed that the words are not changed into Old English - they're all in modern English just without the non-Germanic elements.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:06.080
Another example:

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:08.520
"I onlook the man since he's very ithand."

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.440
Now here's the original sentence I wrote in Standard English:

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:14.140
"I respect the man because he's very industrious."

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:22.760
"Onlook" comes from the Old English "anléc" - 'to observe' or 'to regard' - but it seems the sense has been extended to mean 'regard positively' or 'respect'.

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.920
Now, why did I change the word 'because'?

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:28.060
Well, because the 'cause' - part of 'because' - is of Romance origin.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.580
'Since' is just a Germanic alternative to 'because'.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:36.460
"Ithand" is a real English word which is still used in some English dialects of Northern England and (I believe) also in Scotland.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.220
And, of course, when I speak of that man who's very ithand

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:42.820
I'm referring to a nameknown showplayer I've seen on the farseeer.

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.780
Hm, "a nameknown showplayer I've seen on the farseeer".

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.960
Here's the original phrase in Standard English:

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.740
"A famous actor I've seen on the television."

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.720
"Nameknown" is a newly ashapen (created) word that comes from 'name' and 'known' - markedly.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:59.020
And "showplayer" is another melding (combination) of two bestanding (existing) words.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.340
And "farseeer" is a likeness of the German word "Fernseher".

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.060
In everyday talk, we deal with a lot of Germanic words.

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.440
But for some underwarps (topics) like witship (science) we deal with many more outland (foreign) words.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:16.000
In 1989, author Paul Anderson forlaid a well-known writ named "Uncleftish Beholding"

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.680
- which means 'atomic theory' -

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:20.700
in thoroughly Germanic English without any outland words.

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:22.760
Let's take a look at some of that writ.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:37.120
*see text on screen*

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.620
"Being" is used instead of 'existence'.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.440
"Worldken" means 'physics'.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.000
"Beholding" means 'theory', "stuff" (I believe) means 'matter'

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:50.700
"work" (I think) means 'energy' and "workstead" means 'workplace'.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:04:06.020
*see text on screen*

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.980
"Firststuffs" are elements.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.040
"Sundry" means 'various'.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.260
This is a regular English word, it's just less common than 'various'.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:14.940
"Waterstuff" means 'hydrogen',

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.720
"barest" means 'simplest'

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:18.740
and "ymirstuff" means 'Uranium'.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.200
In my wen (opinion), this seems like English, but somewhat weird English.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:26.380
Anglish is interesting because by trying to read it or use it,

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:31.640
we get a sense of just how much we normally rely on non-Germanic vocabulary when we speak English.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:36.180
On the other hand, you learn that it is possible to speak English without any non-Germanic words

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:40.720
if you're willing to use some archaic vocabulary or new vocabulary based on Germanic roots.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.020
The grammar is the same -

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:45.540
so it's mainly a matter of consciously substituting different vocabulary.

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:47.060
So, the question of the day:

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:49.760
What do you thing of "linguistic purism" like Anglish?

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:53.340
Do you think there's any REASON to remove foreign influences from a language?

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:57.060
Or do you see those influences as just part of the language's development?

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.340
Let us know in the comments down below!

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.040
